{"role":"assistant","content":"Документация показала важные детали: `/oauth/authorize` не требует `response_type`, token-ответ не содержит `refresh_token`/`expires_in`, а scope `CALENDAR:READ/WRITE` валиден. Теперь проверю форум на упоминания `invalid_request`.","tool_calls":[{"type":"function","id":"tool_mrARcOjgcqu9XJDmFrUlK0cu","function":{"name":"WebSearch","arguments":"{\"query\":\"\\\"intervals.icu\\\" oauth authorize \\\"invalid_request\\\" 400 scope\"}"}},{"type":"function","id":"tool_rAIG6UDoPFER7gAFsOx9SbIQ","function":{"name":"FetchURL","arguments":"{\"url\":\"https://forum.intervals.icu/t/intervals-icu-api-integration-cookbook/80090\"}"}}]}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_rAIG6UDoPFER7gAFsOx9SbIQ","content":"The returned content is the main text extracted from the page. If you use it in your answer, cite this page as a markdown link, e.g. [title](url).\n\nThis tells you everything you need to know to get completed activities into and out of Intervals.icu, to download planned workouts and to read and update wellness data. Example calls use curl so you can easily try them out.\n\nSources:\n\n- API access to Intervals.icu\n- Intervals.icu OAuth support\n- Intervals.icu API docs\n\nBy using the API you agree to the Intervals.icu API Terms and Conditions .\n\n## Heading link Authorization\n\nApps need to use an access\\_token obtained via OAuth. If you are just working with your own data call the API using your personal API key (available towards the bottom of the settings page) and basic authentication.\n\n## Heading link Uploading completed activities\n\nThis is done by POSTing multipart/form-data. Example using an access\\_token from OAuth (ACTIVITY:WRITE scope is required):\n\n```lang-auto\n$ curl -F file=@i55610271.fit \\\n    'https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/0/activities?name=Foo&description=Bar' \\\n    -H 'Authorization: Bearer d842c1fc25f241e5ae440d09756448a9'\n\n```\n\nSame example using a personal API key:\n\n```lang-auto\n$ curl -F file=@i55610271.fit \\\n    'https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/0/activities?name=Foo&description=Bar' \\\n    -u API_KEY:6dzmsans11bsyifjxchr87oum\n\n```\n\nThe name and description URL parameters are optional. The file can be fit, tcx or gpx or a zip or gz of the same. There is also an optional external\\_id parameter which you can set to your own activity ID. You can configure an activity URL template for your application and Intervals.icu will use this and the external\\_id to link back to your application from the activity pages.\n\nNote that the athlete id in the path is ‘0’. This indicates that the athlete ID that the access\\_token or API key belongs to should be used.\n\n## Heading link Downloading planned workouts\n\nSee Downloading planned workouts from the API\n\n## Heading link Uploading planned workouts\n\nSee Uploading planned workouts to Intervals.icu\n\n## Heading link Updating wellness data\n\nPush weight, resting HR, HRV, steps etc. using the bulk wellness upload endpoint (WELLNESS:WRITE scope required):\n\n```lang-bash\ncurl -X PUT 'https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/0/wellness-bulk' \\\n    -H 'Authorization: Bearer d842c1fc25f241e5ae440d09756448a9' \\\n    -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n    -d '[{\"id\":\"2024-11-20\",\"weight\": 69.1},{\"id\":\"2024-11-19\",\"weight\": 69.3}]'\n\n```\n\nEach object in the array must have an id (the ISO-8601 local date of the record) and whatever other fields you want to update. See the full docs for a list of possible fields. All units are metric.\n\n## Heading link Downloading wellness data\n\nCall the list wellness endpoint (WELLNESS:READ scope required):\n\n```lang-bash\ncurl 'https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/0/wellness?oldest=2024-11-18&newest=2024-11-20' \\\n    -H 'Authorization: Bearer d842c1fc25f241e5ae440d09756448a9'\n\n```\n\n## Heading link Downloading completed activities\n\nCall the list activities endpoint (ACTIVITY:READ scope is required):\n\n```lang-bash\ncurl 'https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/0/activities?oldest=2024-11-19&newest=2024-11-20' \\\n    -H 'Authorization: Bearer d842c1fc25f241e5ae440d09756448a9'\n\n```\n\nThis returns summary data for each activity. Example abridged response:\n\n```lang-auto\n[{\n  \"id\": \"i55751783\",\n  \"start_date_local\": \"2024-11-20T07:35:18\",\n  \"type\": \"Ride\",\n  ...\n  \"file_type\": \"fit\",\n  ...\n\n```\n\nTo download the original activity file (fit, gpx or tcx) gzip compressed:\n\n```lang-bash\ncurl 'https://intervals.icu/api/v1/activity/i55751783/file' \\\n    -H 'Authorization: Bearer d842c1fc25f241e5ae440d09756448a9' \\\n    > activity.fit.gz\n\n```\n\nTo download the Intervals.icu generated fit file (with edits to activity power etc. and laps matching the intervals):\n\n```lang-bash\ncurl 'https://intervals.icu/api/v1/activity/i55751783/fit-file' \\\n    -H 'Authorization: Bearer d842c1fc25f241e5ae440d09756448a9' \\\n    > activity.fit.gz\n\n```\n\nNote that this is always a fit file even if the original file was a gpx or tcx.\n\n## Heading link User Signup\n\nThe Intervals.icu signup form accepts query parameters to pre-populate the form with data you might already have:\n\n> https://intervals.icu/signup?email=foo&name=bar&ftp=280&max\\_hr=188&sex=M&weight=69.6\n\nWe are considering a paid white label option to allow new users to be created using the API.\n\n## Heading link Webhooks\n\nConfigure webhooks using the management page for your app. Look for your app in /settings and click “Manage App”.\n\nNote that activity webhooks are not delivered for Strava activities.\n\nThe CALENDAR\\_EVENT\\_UPDATED and CALENDAR\\_EVENT\\_DELETED webhooks are legacy. Please use the CALENDAR\\_UPDATED webhook instead.\n\nThe webhook POST payload includes a secret that you can use to verify that the webhook came from Intervals.icu.\n\n```lang-json\n{\n  \"secret\": \"ooKeodacie8I\",\n  \"events\": [\n    {\n      \"athlete_id\": \"...\",\n      \"type\": \"ACTIVITY_UPLOADED\",\n      \"timestamp\": \"2024-12-06T06:40:47.011+00:00\",\n      \"activity\": {...}\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\n```\n\nSome webhooks (e.g. ACTIVITY\\_ANALYZED) are sent after a 60s delay so multiple events for the same activity can be consolidated into one webhook.\n\nYou need to store the Intervals.icu athlete\\_id obtained via OAuth flow so you can map the webhook back to the athlete in your system.\n\nCALENDAR\\_UPDATED webhooks include oauth\\_client\\_id (that created the event if any) and external\\_id (ID supplied when the event was created, if any). You can use this to only look at events created by your app (oauth\\_client\\_id matches your client ID) and quickly update them using the external\\_id.\n\n```lang-auto\n{\n  \"secret\": \"...\",\n  \"events\": [\n    {\n      \"athlete_id\": \"2049151\",\n      \"type\": \"CALENDAR_UPDATED\",\n      \"timestamp\": \"2024-12-18T10:22:16.427+00:00\",\n      \"events\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": 48566307,\n          \"start_date_local\": \"2024-12-20T00:00:00\",\n          \"icu_training_load\": 118,\n          \"type\": \"Ride\",\n          ...\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": 48566308,\n          \"start_date_local\": \"2024-12-21T00:00:00\",\n          \"icu_training_load\": 82,\n          ...\n        }\n      ],\n      \"deleted_events\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\n```\n\nThe SPORT\\_SETTINGS\\_UPDATED webhook is fired when an athletes FTP, zones etc. are changed."}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_mrARcOjgcqu9XJDmFrUlK0cu","content":"Title: Auto-register unknown OAuth client_ids at /authorize instead of returning 400\nSite: Github\nDate: 2026-04-09\nURL: https://github.com/exasol/mcp-server/issues/189\nSnippet: Problem When the OAuth client registry loses a registration (database corruption, container restart, storage migration), clients that don't implement automatic re-registration via DCR get a permanent 400 error. Users see \"Client Not Registered\" with no way to recover except manually removing and re-adding the MCP connector. Observed with ChatGPT's MCP client. Claude Code handles this correctly (re-registers via `POST /register` on `invalid_client`), but ChatGPT does not. Its flow: 1. `POST /toke\n\n---\n\nTitle: Web-how to : FastAPI OAuth2 with Password  returns 400 Bad Request on authorize\nSite: Github\nDate: 2020-06-28\nURL: https://github.com/fastapi/fastapi/issues/1639\nSnippet: Example Here's a self-contained with my use case: From official docs website\n\n---\n\nTitle: 400 bad request for /authorize endpoint - Questions / OAuth/OIDC - Okta Developer Community\nSite: Okta Developer Community\nDate: 2023-06-12\nURL: https://devforum.okta.com/t/400-bad-request-for-authorize-endpoint/24543\nSnippet: post by Mia on Jun 12, 2023 Mia's profile Hello, I am receiving a d “400 bad request” when being redirected to the /authorize endpoint. I made sure that I am passing the right client id and client secret and the application is in active status. This is the request uri: https://dev-MY_ACCOUNT_ID.okta.com/oauth2/default/v1/authorize?client_id=MY_CLIENT_ID&redirect_uri=http%3A%2F%2Flocalhost%3A8080%2Fauthorization-code%2Fcallback&scope=openid+email+profile&state=ApplicationState&nonce=SampleNonce&r\n\n---\n\nTitle: Get \"400 Bad Request\" when attempting to \"authorize account\" cannot use the Trello GitHub Powerup\nSite: Atlassian Community\nDate: 2020-08-27\nURL: https://community.atlassian.com/forums/Trello-questions/Get-quot-400-Bad-Request-quot-when-attempting-to-quot-authorize/qaq-p/1464162\nSnippet: Hi Colin, Thanks for reporting this problem. We've had similar reports before and are working hard to figure out the problem. If you've contacted support, they maybe have asked you to try things like clearing your browser cache, or removing personal settings on the Power-Up and re-authing. If none of those worked, could you try clearing your localstorage for the \"github.trello.services\" domain? I've attached a screenshot of where you can find that on Chrome (should be similar for other browsers \n\n---\n\nTitle: failed to authorize: failed to fetch oauth token: unexpected status from Harbor docker registry: 400 Bad Request\nSite: Stack Overflow\nDate: 2025-01-07\nURL: https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79337293/failed-to-authorize-failed-to-fetch-oauth-token-unexpected-status-from-harbor\nSnippet: The error message you're encountering during my Docker build process indicates an authentication issue with my private registry (it's Harnor): ``` failed to authorize: failed to fetch oauth token: unexpected status from GET request to https://xxx.xxx.com/service/token: 400 Bad Request ``` my gitlab ci scripts ``` stage: build image: registry.internal.amdrnn.ir/library/docker:27.4.1 services: - name: xxx.xxx.com/library/docker:27.4.1-dind alias: docker command: [\"--tls=false\"] # Ensure TLS is dis\n\n---\n\nTitle: Getting 400 bad request when I use \"ValidateAntiForgeryToken\" and \"Authorize\"\nSite: Stack Overflow\nDate: 2017-06-06\nURL: https://stackoverflow.com/questions/44232560/getting-400-bad-request-when-i-use-validateantiforgerytoken-and-authorize\nSnippet: If you are using `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.TagHelpers` it will add a input field with a 'difficult to guess' code: ``` ``` Anyways, even if you add `@Html.AntiForgeryToken()` it wouldn't conflict. However you cannot decorate the 'first' controller action with `[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]`, only the final one with will get the POST. Example: Action 1 ``` [HttpPost] [AllowAnonymous] [ActionName(\"SpecialSignUp\")] public IActionResult Index([FromForm] string email) { // TODO : code in here return Vie\n\n---\n\nTitle: Error 400: invalid_request when trying to authorize account access using OAuth on internal workspace - Google Cloud / Cloud Foundations & Onboarding - Google Developer forums\nSite: Google Developer forums\nDate: 2023-08-15\nURL: https://discuss.google.dev/t/error-400-invalid-request-when-trying-to-authorize-account-access-using-oauth-on-internal-workspace/127390\nSnippet: 1/1 I’m trying to grant access to a python script that updates google sheets documents daily. I was able to get the whole process working without using a Google Workspaces by making an external application in testing mode, but I have to log back in and allow access every couple of days. Since I assumed that was because the app was not in production, I tried to create an internal application with Google Workspaces, but when I go to the authorization page, I get an error code 400, with the error m\n\n---\n\nTitle: PaceTrack Partner API\nSite: PaceTrack\nDate: 2026-08-27\nURL: https://pacetrack.cc/developers?set_lang=ru\nSnippet: GET https://pacetrack.ru/oauth/authorize ... invalid_request со списком errors: [{field, code}] ... Intervals.icu, атлет сам его не сохранял; такие ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: Error 400: invalid_request when trying to authorize account access using OAuth on internal workspace\nSite: Google Cloud Community\nDate: 2023-08-15\nURL: https://www.googlecloudcommunity.com/gc/Google-Cloud-s-Observability/Error-400-invalid-request-when-trying-to-authorize-account/td-p/622091\nSnippet: I'm trying to grant access to a python script that updates google sheets documents daily. I was able to get the whole process working without using a Google Workspaces by making an external application in testing mode, but I have to log back in and allow access every couple of days. Since I assumed that was because the app was not in production, I tried to create an internal application with Google Workspaces, but when I go to the authorization page, I get an error code 400, with the error messa\n\nWhen you rely on a result in your answer, cite it inline as a markdown link, e.g. [title](url)."}
{"role":"assistant","content":"Прямых упоминаний об ошибке 400 нет — посмотрю, как другие open-source интеграции строят authorize URL для Intervals.icu, и проверю поведение публичного эндпоинта.","tool_calls":[{"type":"function","id":"tool_RLuqoezrqnD0c8oRKOGmBpj6","function":{"name":"WebSearch","arguments":"{\"query\":\"github \\\"intervals.icu/oauth/authorize\\\" client_id scope response_type code\"}"}},{"type":"function","id":"tool_vOqs7nETLVeoMMv31sohlKJG","function":{"name":"Bash","arguments":"{\"command\":\"echo '--- A: no response_type, with scopes ---'\\ncurl -s -o /tmp/a.json -w '%{http_code}\\\\n' 'https://intervals.icu/oauth/authorize?client_id=probe-nonexistent&redirect_uri=http%3A%2F%2Flocalhost%3A8766%2Fapi%2Fintervals%2Fcallback&scope=ACTIVITY%3AREAD%2CWELLNESS%3AREAD&state=xyz'; head -c 400 /tmp/a.json; echo\\necho '--- B: response_type=code added ---'\\ncurl -s -o /tmp/b.json -w '%{http_code}\\\\n' 'https://intervals.icu/oauth/authorize?client_id=probe-nonexistent&redirect_uri=http%3A%2F%2Flocalhost%3A8766%2Fapi%2Fintervals%2Fcallback&response_type=code&scope=ACTIVITY%3AREAD%2CWELLNESS%3AREAD&state=xyz'; head -c 400 /tmp/b.json; echo\\necho '--- C: bogus scope ---'\\ncurl -s -o /tmp/c.json -w '%{http_code}\\\\n' 'https://intervals.icu/oauth/authorize?client_id=probe-nonexistent&redirect_uri=http%3A%2F%2Flocalhost%3A8766%2Fapi%2Fintervals%2Fcallback&scope=BOGUS%3AREAD&state=xyz'; head -c 400 /tmp/c.json; echo\",\"timeout\":60}"}}]}
